    </category>
  </question>
  <question type="multichoice">
    <name>
      <text>ALG2_U04_L04_GP_Q01</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Question ID:</strong> ALG2_U04_L04_GP_Q01</p><p><strong>MLA Standard:</strong> MLA.A2.LOG.2</p><p>What is log_2(16)?</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <defaultgrade>1.0000000</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.3333333</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <single>true</single>
    <shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers>
    <answernumbering>abc</answernumbering>
    <answer fraction="100" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[4]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[Correct. The logarithm asks what power of 2 equals 16, and 2⁴ = 16.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[2]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[2&#178; = 4, not 16.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[8]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[2⁸ = 256, not 16.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[32]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[This is not an exponent that makes 2 equal 16.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
  </question>
  <question type="multichoice">
    <name>
      <text>ALG2_U04_L04_GP_Q02</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Question ID:</strong> ALG2_U04_L04_GP_Q02</p><p><strong>MLA Standard:</strong> MLA.A2.LOG.2</p><p>Which equation is equivalent to log_3(27)=3?</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <defaultgrade>1.0000000</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.3333333</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <single>true</single>
    <shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers>
    <answernumbering>abc</answernumbering>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[27&#179; = 3]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[This reverses the base and input.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="100" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[3&#179; = 27]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[Correct. A logarithm statement rewrites as base raised to output equals input.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[3 + 27 = 30]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[Logarithms are about exponents, not addition.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[27/3 = 9]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[Division does not represent the logarithmic relationship.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
  </question>
  <question type="multichoice">
    <name>
      <text>ALG2_U04_L04_GP_Q03</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Question ID:</strong> ALG2_U04_L04_GP_Q03</p><p><strong>MLA Standard:</strong> MLA.A2.LOG.2</p><p>What is the domain of y = log_5(x)?</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <defaultgrade>1.0000000</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.3333333</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <single>true</single>
    <shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers>
    <answernumbering>abc</answernumbering>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[all real numbers]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[Logarithmic inputs must be positive.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[x &gt;= 0]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[x = 0 is not included because no positive base power equals 0.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="100" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[x &gt; 0]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[Correct. A basic logarithmic function only accepts positive inputs.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[x != 5]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[The base does not create a domain restriction like a denominator.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
  </question>
  <question type="multichoice">
    <name>
      <text>ALG2_U04_L04_GP_Q04</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Question ID:</strong> ALG2_U04_L04_GP_Q04</p><p><strong>MLA Standard:</strong> MLA.A2.LOG.2</p><p>Which statement describes logarithms?</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <defaultgrade>1.0000000</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.3333333</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <single>true</single>
    <shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers>
    <answernumbering>abc</answernumbering>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[A logarithm gives a slope.]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[Slope is a rate of change, not the meaning of a logarithm.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[A logarithm gives a y-intercept.]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[A logarithm can have intercepts, but that is not its definition.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[A logarithm gives a denominator restriction.]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[That confuses logs with rational expressions.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="100" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[A logarithm gives an exponent.]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[Correct. log_b(a) asks what exponent on b produces a.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
  </question>
  <question type="multichoice">
    <name>
      <text>ALG2_U04_L04_GP_Q05</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Question ID:</strong> ALG2_U04_L04_GP_Q05</p><p><strong>MLA Standard:</strong> MLA.A2.LOG.2</p><p>The graph of y = log_2(x) is the inverse of which graph?</p><p><img src="@@PLUGINFILE@@/ALG2_U04_L04_GP_Q05_graph.png" alt="Parabola graph supports quadratic, vertex, zero, boundary, or shading interpretation." style="max-width:100%;height:auto;" /></p>]]></text>
      <file name="ALG2_U04_L04_GP_Q05_graph.png" path="/" encoding="base64"></file>
    </questiontext>
    <defaultgrade>1.0000000</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.3333333</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <single>true</single>
    <shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers>
    <answernumbering>abc</answernumbering>
    <answer fraction="100" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[y = 2ˣ]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[Correct. Logarithmic and exponential functions with the same base are inverses.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[y = x&#178;]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[A quadratic is not the matching exponential inverse.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[y = x/2]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[This is linear.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[y = 2x]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[This is linear, not exponential.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
  </question>
</quiz>